    {
      "id": "trumpet",
      "title": "Trumpet: the high pitch and the breath",
      "question": "Eight bars of tongued trumpet lead alone: which sounds most like a trumpet imitation, and where is the very high pitch?",
      "notes": "Your phase 4a note on c03: 'very good; an occasional very high pitch'. Measured: the monk's breath is two fixed high partials (3.8 and 5 kHz) that make whichever harmonic meets them ring, most on the A4s (its 11th harmonic, 4.8 kHz, stands out by 18 dB). The profile breathes at 0.75 all through each note; c03 breathed at 0.45 with a strong puff at each onset. Less breath makes the whistle quieter (0.3 is about Gervill's level); only 'no breath' removes it, and may sound too pure. Sung as written (A4-F5 sit within your D37 range). Every clip at Gervill's loudness; blind mode is worth it.",
