    {
      "id": "sax",
      "title": "Tenor sax: brightness",
      "question": "Eight bars of tenor sax alone (mostly slurred, E3-G4): which sounds most like a tenor sax imitation?",
      "notes": "Your phase 4a note on c04: 'too bright, wouldn't use this for a tenor sax'. The profile is already darker than c04 was (u drifting to o instead of to a, a bigger voice). Measured: it is no brighter than Gervill's tenor overall, but it misses the warm middle (800-1600 Hz) and its third formant (2.2-2.9 kHz) stands alone above that hole. 'F3 lower' moves that formant down into the hole (the closest match to Gervill's spectrum); 'F3 off' removes it (darker than Gervill); less breath turns down the two high breath partials. The last clip also gives the tenor the softer attack and the longer 'o' release the sax profiles were meant to have (the tenor lost them by accident). Every clip at Gervill's loudness.",
